      "body": "## Bill — design note v0: cards → semantics → (scorer factor, rule bidder, profile match)\n\nProposal for the bidding side of \"a player arrives with a convention card\". For the lead to cut or reshape; I am starting the prototype on `gen9/bill-card-semantics` now since it is cheap to redirect.\n\n### 1. One compiled artifact, three consumers\n\nA card is compiled once into **call meanings**: for a context (seat, vulnerability, relative auction so far) an ordered list of `(call, predicate, priority)`. A predicate is a disjunction of clauses (Polish 1♣ and Multi 2♦ need OR); a clause is intervals on hand features: HCP, four suit lengths, balanced / semi-balanced, suit quality (top-3/top-5 honours), stoppers, controls, and length relations (♥ ≥ ♠). Every feature is computable from the hand types the scorer already uses (lengths + AKQJT bits), so everything vectorizes over the scorer's layout pool.\n\n- **(a) Card factor (post-filter).** Noisy-rule likelihood of the observed call: `P(c | hand) = (1−ε)·1[first satisfied rule = c] + ε·softmax(−λ·dist(hand, clause))[c]`. It gives positive inference (the call's own constraints) and negative inference (the hand did not satisfy a higher-priority call, e.g. a third-seat pass). Contexts the card does not cover contribute 0, so the net keeps everything past the declared layer. Interface = `FitScorer.type_log_score(length, hon, seat, dealer, vul, auction)`; combine as `log w = a·scorer + b·card`, `a, b` fitted by held-out log-loss of the true hand type.\n- **(b) Rule bidder.** Same artifact run deterministically, implementing `system_bidders.RuleBidder`. Covered contexts by rule; uncovered contexts by a fallback (first: a house net bidder; later: natural placement from the ranges each side has shown, which the compiled meanings give directly). Purpose: a generator for systems we have no bidder for (EPBot is out), so system-aware nets get training data from genuinely different systems. A net bidder can then imitate it on covered contexts and the existing bidding loop can fix the continuations.\n- **(c) Profile matching.** Behavioural distance, not field distance: sample hands per covered context, weighted by how often the context arises, and measure how often two profiles call differently. Works card-vs-card and card-vs-net (run the net bidder on the card's covered contexts). Output: nearest profiles with distances, plus the **residual** (contexts where the card and the profile's net disagree), which is exactly where the card factor should carry weight even when a profile matches.\n\n### 2. Deviation as a parameter, per player\n\n`ε` and `λ` are per constraint family (NT range, suit length, opening strength, preempt discipline...) and per player. Defaults from a population prior; updated from that player's records (a hand that showed out of range raises ε for that family) — the \"this player might have done this\" experience base on the bidding side. Same shape as signalling honesty (Sam), so I propose we share one per-player record of `{family: (agree, deviate)}` counts.\n\n### 3. Card ingest\n\nSchema: JSON descriptor with ACBL/WBF card fields (general approach, NT ranges by seat/vul, major/minor lengths, 2-level openings, responses to 1NT/1M, competitive doubles, overcall ranges, defence to 1NT, slam conventions; carding fields already on the Gen 8 branch). Ingest: an LLM fills the descriptor from a PDF/image/text card, then deterministic validation and a round-trip summary shown back to the player for confirmation. The compiler only reads the validated descriptor.\n\n### 4. First systems to encode (as cards, no external engine)\n\nSAYC, 2/1 GF, Acol (12–14, four-card majors), Precision (16+ 1♣, nebulous 1♦, 2♣ natural), Polish Club (multi-way 1♣: the disjunction test). Plus the house profiles described by their behaviour, not a card.\n\n### 5. Tests / done-when for the prototype\n\n- Compiler round-trip: each card's rule bidder reproduces its own card on generated hands (100% on covered contexts).\n- Card factor: on deals bid by a card's rule bidder, log-loss of the true hand type falls when the factor is added to the system-blind scorer, and *rises* when the wrong card is declared (\"wrong must cost\").\n- Profile distance: house-gen6 vs house-gen5 by this measure in the same order as the measured 26% auction disagreement; SAYC vs 2/1 small, SAYC vs Precision large.\n\n### Questions for the lead\n\n1. Is the card factor the right integration point for \"the card post-filters the net\", or do you want the card as an input to an MoE gate in the scorer/bidder (the Gen 8 `sys_gate` path)? I can feed both: the compiled meanings can also produce a feature vector per call.\n2. Do you want me to own the bidder side of the MoE (system-conditioned bidder net) too, or only cards/semantics/generators?\n3. Compute: the prototype needs no box. A generator data run and a scorer retrain later would want one box for a few hours; I will ask before taking anything.\n\n— Bill\n\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_"
    },
